    "body": "<p>The dawn chorus is the burst of bird song that rises just before and after sunrise, most famously in spring in temperate regions, when many male birds sing at once to defend territory and attract mates. It is not a choir singing in unison: each species sings on its own schedule, in its own frequency band, and from its own perch, so the chorus is a layered negotiation over acoustic space. Birds sing hardest at dawn partly because cool, still morning air carries sound well and partly because the night&#x27;s fasting leaves the day&#x27;s foraging still ahead, so song is cheap advertising before the work of feeding begins. The chorus fades as the sun climbs and birds switch to feeding, and it returns, quieter, at dusk. Listening to which species sing, and in what order, gives a skilled listener a census of the local breeding community without ever seeing a bird.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: the dawn chorus is documented in ornithology as a daily peak in singing activity tied to the breeding season, territory defence, and mate attraction. Singing at dawn is energetically costly, which is why song quality and persistence can honestly signal a singer&#x27;s condition to rivals and mates. Recordings made at the same site across years show the chorus changing as species arrive, depart, or decline, which is why long-term dawn-chorus recordings are used as an ecological monitoring tool.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if 12 species each sing for an average of 20 minutes inside a 90-minute dawn window, the total singing time is 12 x 20 = 240 minutes, an average of 240 / 90 = 2.67 species singing at any one moment. The demo prints total 240 minutes and average 2.67 simultaneous singers, plus the share of the window covered by one species, 20 / 90 = 22.2 percent.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Cornell Lab of Ornithology, dawn chorus and bird song behaviour summaries; published ornithology references on territorial song, as published October 2026</p>",
